+# Where to look for the FAA Red Team disclosure follow-up
+
+Public derivative of the November 10, 2026 source review · B129 / PD02–PD04 custody comparison. This addendum examines the public-file route for OSC disclosure DI-02-0207. The May 2003 answer investigated in CC-2006-085 / 042R0122001 remains a separate case with its own correction and administrative-action trail. [November 6 comparator and source links](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/tree/main/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-osc-response-record-chain-2026-11-06) · [DOT OIG Commission referral report, PDF pp. 12–13](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12)
+
+## Observations
+
+| Source and exact location | What the record says |
+| --- | --- |
+| OSC FY 2003 annual report, printed pp. 13–14 and endnote 28, PDF pp. 19–20, 31 | The report describes the public file at OSC. For the Red Team referral, it says the investigation found gross mismanagement and failure to use testing findings. OSC found the investigation reasonable and the statement of planned correction sufficient, and separately found the accountability response inadequate. Endnote 28 says the disclosure was referred in February 2002 and closed in March 2003. This is the agency's account of the same referral described in the March 18 letter. [Annual report](https://osc.gov/~assets/docs/fy-2003-annual-report-to-congress.pdf#page=19) |
+| March 18, 2003 OSC letter, archive packet PDF pp. 16–17 | The letter describes the January 30 inquiry and the February 24 management reply. It says OSC put the redacted reply and the OIG report in its public file and sent enclosures to the President and committee chairs. It records closure of the disclosure and separately describes the retaliation matter as pending. [Letter](https://911digitalarchive.org/files/original/5f2632a01d18e904772878d3e9d9ce25.PDF#page=16) |
+| N1-481-90-001, PDF p. 3, item 2(c), compared with p. 1, rows 2(a)–2(d) | The body describes an OSC public file of sanitized correspondence for matters referred to agency heads and directs it to stay out of the Federal Records Center and NARA transfer routes. The cover labels item 2(c) “No disposition instruction.” The cover also records that the proposed official-file and automated-record rules at 2(a)–2(b) were lined off before approval and refers to NC1-146-85-01. The diagonal strikeouts are visible on the scan. Preserve the body and cover observations at their respective scopes. [1990 schedule body](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0481/n1-481-90-001_sf115.pdf#page=3) · [Cover](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0481/n1-481-90-001_sf115.pdf#page=1) |
+| NC1-146-85-01, PDF pp. 1, 3–4 | The cover lists disclosure items 2/[a] and 2/[b] as “Active (T).” The linked historical schedule describes the whistleblower files and gives separate office-file and automated-file retention instructions. The office-file text specifies retirement one year after closure and destruction three years after closure. The page gives a class rule. Its application and any actual transfer or disposal for DI-02-0207 remain unverified. [Schedule](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0146/nc1-146-85-01_sf115.pdf#page=3) |
+| 2003 schedule, PDF pp. 1, 7, compared with the 1985 cover and RG 481 index | Item 16 distinguishes permanent significant litigation/investigative cases from temporary other cases. The 1985 cover's supersession entry is on item 1/[a]. The 2003 PDF cover reads `N1-481-03-0001`. The RG 481 index and 1985 cross-reference read `N1-481-03-001`. Preserve both identifiers. The documents examined leave the actual classification of DI-02-0207 unverified. [2003 schedule](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0481/n1-481-03-001_sf115.pdf#page=7) · [Cover](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0481/n1-481-03-001_sf115.pdf#page=1) · [RG 481 index](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/index.html?dir=independent-agencies%2Frg-0481) |
+
+The prior review preserved OIG's reported corrective steps alongside OSC's uncertainty about whether procedures were developed or planned. OIG reported that it did not substantiate the specific deliberate-suppression allegations. The annual report's account supplies the agency's published description of the referral and closure. The original reply, implementation record and any actual accountability decision remain open. [August 2002 OIG report, PDF pp. 4–5, 12](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/kaplan020816.pdf#page=4) · [Annual report, printed p. 14](https://osc.gov/~assets/docs/fy-2003-annual-report-to-congress.pdf#page=20)
+
+## Access and file structure
+
+- The legacy OSC annual-report endpoint returned HTTP 200 with PDF bytes. The other OSC annual-report URL returned HTTP 404 and an HTML body. Both responses were preserved with exact URLs, dates, MIME values and SHA-256 hashes. They establish two retrieval outcomes.
+- The held archive packet produced `PDF document, version 1.6, 10 pages` in `file`. PyMuPDF, pypdf and Poppler each reported 17. Pypdf identified root Pages object 2 with child Pages objects 77 and 118, whose `/Count` values and immediate child counts were 10 and 7 respectively. The test preserves the prior discrepancy and locates both counts in the same bytes. The reason for the utility difference and the completeness of the historical enclosure set remain unverified. [Archive packet](https://911digitalarchive.org/files/original/5f2632a01d18e904772878d3e9d9ce25.PDF)
+- The downloaded collection 293 landing page contained 20 unique numeric item links, including 97085 for the OSC letter. The bounded title screen did not identify a separately listed February 24 reply. The screen covered one collection page and the already-held letter. [Collection 293](https://911digitalarchive.org/collections/293/) · [Item 97085](https://911digitalarchive.org/items/97085/)
+
+## Unresolved records
+
+- **DI-02-0207.** Seek the filed redacted February 24 reply and a public-file index or item-level custody entry first. Keep the January 30 inquiry, complete enclosure set, recipient-side receipts, implementation evidence and actual personnel decision separately open. A copy in an executive or congressional accession would require an accession or enclosure match.
+- **CC-2006-085 / PD01–PD04.** The corrected May 2003 answer and receipt, signed consideration/action/no-action determination, and dated response and implementation or closure for recommendations 1 and 2 remain missing. The OSC disclosure file supplies a concrete comparator for separating official copies, public copies and recipients. An equivalent routing or scheduling rule for the Commission-referral case remains unverified. Recommendations 3 and 4 on the report's next page remain separate. [DOT OIG report, PDF pp. 12–13](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12)
+
+Reading scope covered the cited pages and relevant item rows, catalog titles and the held packet's PDF structure. Source pins record the bounded checks and conflicting observations. No item-level transfer, disposal, personnel action, corrected-answer delivery or recommendation implementation was authenticated in this pass.
